---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const faqSchema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  mainEntity: [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "Am I eligible for a Wolverhampton private hire licence?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "You must be at least 21 years old, have held a full UK driving licence for at least 12 months, have the right to work in the UK, pass a Group 2 medical examination, and pass an enhanced DBS (criminal record) check.",
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "What is a Certificate of Good Conduct?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "A Certificate of Good Conduct (also accepted as a Police Character/Conduct Certificate) is required if you have lived or worked outside the UK for 3 or more continuous months since the age of 18, including British citizens. It covers the period you spent abroad, because a UK DBS check cannot, and it must be provided in English.",
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "Do I need to join the DBS Update Service?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "It is not compulsory, but it affects your licence. If you are not registered on the DBS Update Service you will only receive a six-month licence, and you will need to apply for a new DBS certificate to get your next licence term.",
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "What check codes do I need for a Wolverhampton taxi licence?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "Two: a 9-digit HMRC Tax Check code, obtained from gov.uk, and a Right to Work code, also obtained from gov.uk. Both are submitted through the council's Provide a Check Code page. Every licensed driver and operator must be registered to pay tax and prove their right to work.",
      },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      name: "What happens if my doctor refuses to complete my medical certificate?",
      acceptedAnswer: {
        "@type": "Answer",
        text: "You can visit any other GMC-registered medical practitioner to have it completed. Note that if you are not medically fit to drive to Group 2 standard, a driver licence application will be refused.",
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Am I Eligible for a Wolverhampton PHV Licence? Full Requirements"
  description="Everything you need to qualify for a Wolverhampton private hire licence — age, Certificate of Good Conduct, DBS, and vehicle requirements."
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(faqSchema)} />

  <main class="md:flex md:justify-center">
    <article class="prose prose-lg max-w-[750px]">
      <h1>
        Am I Eligible for a Wolverhampton PHV Licence? Full Requirements
      </h1>

      <p>
        Before you spend any money, it's worth checking whether you're
        <strong>eligible for a Wolverhampton private hire licence</strong>.
        This page sets out the requirements in plain English — your age and
        driving licence, the medical, the DBS check, the check codes you'll
        need, the Certificate of Good Conduct for time spent abroad, and
        the basics on vehicles. When you're ready to start, our guide on
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/how-to-apply/"
          >how to apply for a Wolverhampton private hire licence</a
        >
        walks through the process step by step.
      </p>

      <h2>Driver requirements at a glance</h2>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Requirement</th>
            <th>What you need</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Age</td>
            <td>At least 21 years old</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Driving licence</td>
            <td>A full UK driving licence held for at least 12 months</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Right to work</td>
            <td>Evidence of your right to work in the UK, plus a check code</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Medical</td>
            <td>A Group 2 medical, completed on the D4 form</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Criminal record check</td>
            <td>An enhanced DBS check</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Tax</td>
            <td>A 9-digit HMRC Tax Check code</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Time spent abroad</td>
            <td>
              A Certificate of Good Conduct, if you've lived or worked
              outside the UK for 3 or more continuous months since age 18
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p>
        If any of these might be an issue — for example points on your
        licence or a past conviction — check the council's official guidance
        before you apply, as decisions on individual cases rest with the
        council.
      </p>

      <h2>What is the Certificate of Good Conduct?</h2>

      <p>
        A UK DBS check can only cover time you've spent in the UK. So if
        you've lived or worked abroad, you'll also need a
        <strong>Certificate of Good Conduct</strong> — the council also
        accepts a "Police Character/Conduct Certificate", and the same
        document is sometimes called a Certificate of Good Character —
        covering that period abroad. If you've spent time outside the UK,
        this is usually the most confusing part of the application, so here's
        exactly what's required.
      </p>

      <h3>When do I need one?</h3>

      <p>
        You need one if you've <strong>lived or worked outside the UK for
        three or more continuous months since the age of 18</strong>.
      </p>

      <p>
        This applies <strong>even to British citizens</strong>. If you're a
        British citizen who went abroad — for example on an extended holiday
        — for more than three months, you still need the certificate,
        provided you were over 18 when you returned to the UK.
      </p>

      <h3>Does it have to be in English?</h3>

      <p>
        Yes. It must be provided in English. It's usually pre-translated, but
        if it isn't, you'll need to obtain a <strong>notarised
        translation</strong> at your own expense before the council can
        verify it and issue your licence.
      </p>

      <h3>What if my home country writes my name in a different order?</h3>

      <p>
        You'll need to show proof that you legally changed your name, or the
        certificate must be in your current name.
      </p>

      <h3>I provided one last year — do I need a new one?</h3>

      <p>
        No — provided you submitted it when you applied last year and you
        haven't spent more than three consecutive months abroad since.
      </p>

      <h3>It's taking a long time, it's expensive, or the country won't issue it</h3>

      <p>
        The council can't take responsibility for delays or costs caused by
        third parties — these need to be raised with the relevant embassy or
        diplomatic service. Where a country won't issue a certificate because
        you're not a citizen, the council will need to be satisfied you've
        exhausted all possible avenues before it can proceed.
      </p>

      <p>
        Because of those delays, work it out early: add up any single stretch
        abroad of three months or more, at any point since your 18th
        birthday, and start the process before you submit your application
        rather than after.
      </p>

      <h3>Can I use my British passport as proof I was granted asylum?</h3>

      <p>
        No. A British passport is not accepted as proof of a successful
        asylum claim.
      </p>

      <h2>Do I need to be on the DBS Update Service?</h2>

      <p>
        You don't have to be — but it directly affects the length of your
        licence:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Not registered on the Update Service?</strong> You'll only
          receive a <strong>six-month licence</strong>, and you'll need to
          apply for a new DBS certificate to get your next licence term.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Registered and then came off it?</strong> You'll need to
          provide a new DBS certificate. The council will email you with what
          is required.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        For renewals, your DBS certificate must be no older than 12 months
        from the date your former licence expired. Basic, standard and
        enhanced certificates are all accepted at renewal, as is current
        registration on the DBS Update Service.
      </p>

      <h2>What check codes do I need?</h2>

      <p>
        Every licensed driver and operator must be registered to pay tax and
        able to prove their right to work. That means two codes:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>HMRC Tax Check code</strong> — a 9-digit code from gov.uk
          (search for "complete a tax check for a taxi, private hire or scrap
          metal licence").
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Right to Work code</strong> — also from gov.uk (search for
          "prove your right to work").
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        Both are submitted through the council's "Provide a Check Code" page.
        Missing codes are one of the most common reasons an application or
        renewal stalls.
      </p>

      <h2>What are the medical requirements?</h2>

      <p>
        You must meet the <strong>Group 2 medical standard</strong> — the
        higher standard that applies to professional drivers such as bus and
        lorry drivers — with the certificate completed on the
        <strong>D4 form</strong>. If you are not medically fit to drive to
        Group 2 standard, a driver licence application will be refused.
      </p>

      <p>
        If your own doctor declines to complete the form, you can visit
        <strong>any other GMC-registered medical practitioner</strong> to
        have it done. Once licensed, medicals are repeated
        <strong>every 5 years for drivers aged 45 and over</strong>, and
        <strong>every year for drivers aged 65 and over</strong>.
      </p>

      <h2>Is driver training required?</h2>

      <p>
        Yes — the knowledge test is taken alongside a training course. There
        is also a re-training requirement for existing drivers: anyone who
        completed Worcestershire County Council's Driver Training Programme
        <strong>before 19 September 2019</strong> is being asked to
        re-attend, because the updated course adds new conditions of licence
        and County Lines content.
      </p>

      <p>
        Arrange it directly with Worcestershire County Council
        (<a href="mailto:taxidrivertraining@worcestershire.gov.uk"
          >taxidrivertraining@worcestershire.gov.uk</a
        >) as soon as possible, to avoid renewal delays. You'll need to give
        your name, National Insurance number, driving licence number, email,
        phone number and licence number.
      </p>

      <p>
        For the test itself, see our guides on
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/how-to-pass/"
          >how to pass the Wolverhampton PHV test</a
        >
        and
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/8-key-questions/">the 8 key questions</a>.
        You can also drill the topics until they're automatic in the
        <a
          href="https://apps.apple.com/gb/app/phv-prep-uk-taxi-badge-test/id6776364932"
          target="_blank"
          rel="noopener">PHV Prep UK app</a
        >.
      </p>

      <h2>Which licence do I need, and where can I work?</h2>

      <p>
        This is one of the most misunderstood areas, so it's worth being
        precise:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Private hire driver licence</strong> — you can accept
          pre-booked journeys anywhere in the country, as long as the journey
          is in a Wolverhampton-licensed private hire vehicle and booked
          through a private hire operator licensed by Wolverhampton. You
          <strong>cannot</strong> ply for hire or use ranks: private hire is
          pre-booked only.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Hackney Carriage (dual) driver licence</strong> — you can
          ply or stand for hire (use a taxi rank) in a
          Wolverhampton-licensed Hackney Carriage anywhere within
          Wolverhampton, and you can also accept pre-booked journeys anywhere
          in the country.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What are the vehicle requirements?</h2>

      <p>
        If you're licensing a vehicle as well, it must meet
        <strong>all</strong> of the following:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>It cannot already be licensed by another authority</strong>
          — you must surrender any licence held elsewhere first.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No more than 9 seats including the driver</strong>, with
          passenger doors that open on both sides.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>There is no minimum or maximum engine size.</strong>
        </li>
        <li><strong>It can be any colour.</strong></li>
        <li>
          <strong>Insurance write-off categories.</strong> Category S
          (formerly Cat C) or N (formerly Cat D) is acceptable only if the
          vehicle has been repaired and passed an MOT. Categories A and B are
          prohibited.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Only manufacturer, factory-fitted privacy glass</strong> is
          allowed — no after-market tints.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>LPG vehicles</strong> need an LPG certificate, and the
          logbook must state that the vehicle is LPG.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Vehicle age and licence length</h3>

      <p>
        A vehicle <strong>over 10 years old</strong> will only receive a
        <strong>6-month licence</strong> rather than a full term.
      </p>

      <p>
        For vehicles <strong>over 12 years old with emissions under 75g
        CO₂</strong>, there's a separate application route: send all pages of
        your logbook to Vehicle Licensing, and if it meets the requirements
        you'll be sent an application form.
      </p>

      <h3>Does a new vehicle need an MOT?</h3>

      <p>
        If the vehicle is brand new with only delivery mileage (a maximum of
        500 miles), no MOT certificate is required.
      </p>

      <h3>Other common vehicle questions</h3>

      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Someone else can drive your licensed vehicle</strong>,
          provided they are insured and hold a relevant Wolverhampton-issued
          driver licence.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Personalised number plates are allowed</strong> — see the
          council's "Apply for private registration" page.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>No smartphone for walkaround checks?</strong> Print and
          complete the government's driver vehicle condition checklist
          instead.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>To surrender a vehicle licence</strong>, send the licence,
          plate, any stickers and a completed surrender form to City of
          Wolverhampton Council, Chapel Street, Bilston, Wolverhampton WV14
          0PH.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Where to go next</h2>

      <p>
        Once you know you're eligible, read
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/how-to-apply/"
          >how to apply for a Wolverhampton private hire licence</a
        >
        for the step-by-step process, and our
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/guide/licensing-faqs/"
          >Wolverhampton PHV licensing FAQs</a
        >
        for the practical day-to-day questions — renewals, badges, the
        MyLicence Portal and more. You can also learn more about the app on
        the <a href="/phv-prep-uk">PHV Prep UK page</a>, get help via
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/support">support</a>, or read our
        <a href="/phv-prep-uk/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        <strong>PHV Prep UK is an independent study aid.</strong> It is not
        affiliated with, authorised by, or endorsed by City of Wolverhampton
        Council or any licensing authority. Always check the council's
        official guidance for the latest eligibility requirements and fees.
      </p>
    </article>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
